## 🎯 Action-Driven Development Guidelines

### When to Create Actions

- **Complex Business Logic**: When controller methods become too complex
- **Reusable Operations**: When the same logic is needed in multiple places
- **Clear Business Operations**: For well-defined business processes
- **Testing Requirements**: When you need to test business logic in isolation

### When NOT to Use Actions

- **Simple CRUD Operations**: Basic create/read/update/delete can stay in controllers
- **One-line Operations**: Don't over-engineer simple operations
- **Framework Conventions**: When Laravel conventions are sufficient

### Action Testing

```php
// Example Action Test
it('creates a user with valid data', function () {
    $action = new CreateUserAction();
    $userData = [
        'name' => 'John Doe',
        'email' => 'john@example.com',
        'password' => 'password123'
    ];
    
    $user = $action->execute($userData);
    
    expect($user)->toBeInstanceOf(User::class);
    expect($user->email)->toBe('john@example.com');
});
```

**Remember**: As a senior architect, your responsibility is to deliver high-quality, maintainable code that serves as a reference for other developers. Focus on the Action-Driven Development pattern as the main architectural approach for this Laravel application.
